## Design

Change render_input dict-form path to detect render_for_prompt returning BaseModel and flatten fields.

### render_input change (renderers.py)

Replace the dict comprehension with a loop:

```python
if isinstance(input_data, dict):
    result = {}
    for k, v in input_data.items():
        rendered, extra_fields = _render_with_flattening(v, renderer)
        result[k] = rendered
        for fname, fval in extra_fields.items():
            if fname not in result:  # dont overwrite existing keys
                result[fname] = fval
    return result
```

### New helper: _render_with_flattening

Returns (rendered_value, extra_fields_dict). extra_fields is empty unless
render_for_prompt() returned a BaseModel.

```python
def _render_with_flattening(value, renderer):
    if hasattr(value, "render_for_prompt") and callable(value.render_for_prompt):
        result = value.render_for_prompt()
        if isinstance(result, BaseModel):
            whole = renderer.render(result) if renderer else describe_value(result)
            fields = {}
            for fname, finfo in result.__class__.model_fields.items():
                if finfo.exclude:
                    continue
                fval = getattr(result, fname)
                fields[fname] = _render_single(fval, renderer)
            return whole, fields
        if isinstance(result, str):
            return result, {}
        return result, {}
    return _render_single(value, renderer), {}
```

Risk: LOW. Only fires when render_for_prompt() returns BaseModel in dict-form input.

## Root Cause
Construct.__init__ (construct.py:102-121) iterates self.nodes and directly assigns item.llm_config and item.renderer. Since Node's model_config isn't frozen, the mutation succeeds — but it mutates the original objects passed to the constructor, violating immutable IR.

The comment on line 106 says 'use model_copy' but the code does the opposite.

## Fix Approach
Replace direct assignment with model_copy + list replacement:
```python
for i, item in enumerate(self.nodes):
    updates = {}
    if hasattr(item, 'llm_config'):
        updates['llm_config'] = {**self.llm_config, **item.llm_config}
    if hasattr(item, 'renderer') and getattr(item, 'renderer', None) is None and self.renderer is not None:
        updates['renderer'] = self.renderer
    if updates:
        self.nodes[i] = item.model_copy(update=updates)
```
Single pass, one model_copy per node (not two).

description:
make_subgraph_fn only passes node_id and neo_subgraph_input to the sub-construct. When nodes inside a sub-construct declare context=['build_catalog'], the field doesn't exist in the sub-construct's state — it's in the parent.

The sub-construct factory should inspect nodes inside it for context= declarations and forward those fields from parent state into the sub-construct input.

Current: sub_input = {'node_id': ..., 'neo_subgraph_input': ...}
Needed:  sub_input = {'node_id': ..., 'neo_subgraph_input': ..., 'build_catalog': parent_state['build_catalog']}

Without this, piarch works around it by loading the catalog from disk in the prompt compiler when the context value is None.

Discovered during qtfof.4's trace-similar step (spec_types._structural_type_name hash collision fix).

register_type(name, cls)'s idempotency check (_fields_match, spec_types.py:45-47) is:

    def _fields_match(a, b):
        return set(a.model_fields.keys()) == set(b.model_fields.keys())

This only compares FIELD NAMES, never field TYPES. So two structurally DIFFERENT models sharing the same field name set (e.g. Bag(items: list[Tagged]) and Claims(items: list[str])) are treated as 'same schema' and register_type silently no-ops on the second call, keeping the FIRST-registered (wrong-shaped) class -- this was the SECOND half of qtfof.4's bug: even after the hash-collision half is fixed (_structural_type_name now hashes item types, so genuinely different shapes get different registry keys and never reach this check for the qtfof.4 repro specifically), any caller of register_type() with an EXPLICIT name (e.g. loader.py's agent_spec_properties_to_types(props, name=<caller-supplied>) path, not the structural-name path) can still trip this: two different-typed-same-named-fields models registered under the SAME explicit name silently collapse to the first one.

FIX: _fields_match should compare (field_name, field_type) pairs, not just field_name, so a genuine type mismatch under a reused explicit name warns/overwrites correctly instead of silently keeping the wrong class.

